## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical virtual memory implementation skills including COW semantics, page table management, reference counting, synchronization, and TLB consistency. It evaluates the ability to handle concurrent page faults without memory leaks or race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Copy-On-Write (COW) optimization
- Page table updates and TLB invalidation
- Race condition handling and synchronization primitives
- Concurrent memory management

## Code Example
```c
void handle_write_fault(PTE *pte) {
  acquire(pte->lock);
  if (pte->refcount > 1) {
    Page *newpage = alloc_page();
    memcpy(newpage->addr, pte->frame->addr, PAGE_SIZE);
    pte->frame = newpage;
    pte->writable = 1;
    pte->refcount--;
    invalidate_tlb(pte->vaddr);
  } else {
    pte->writable = 1;
  }
  release(pte->lock);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Beginners should reason about virtual memory protection, per-thread isolation, and fault handling without requiring full OS support. Guard pages are a simple, practical mechanism to detect overflows early.

## Key Concepts

- Virtual memory protection and per-thread stacks
- Guard page placement and page faults
- Graceful thread termination and resource cleanup
- Basic testing for concurrency and fault paths

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal skeleton
typedef struct { void *stack; void *guard; } Thread;

void thread_init(Thread *t) {
  t->stack = mmap(NULL, 8*1024*1024, PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE,
                  MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANONYMOUS, -1, 0);
  t->guard = (char*)t->stack - PAGE_SIZE;
  mprotect(t->guard, PAGE_SIZE, PROT_NONE);
}

void fault_handler(void *addr, Thread *t) {
  if (addr >= t->guard && addr < (char*)t->guard + PAGE_SIZE) {
    terminate_thread(t);
  }
}
``` 

